use std
into vis_frame.amni

Imports: os, gfx, math, gfx.linalg, gfx.gltf, gfx.envmap and gfx.scene3d.

`fn num(s const byte[:]) f32` parses a real with `parse_flt` (0 when it does not parse) and converts it `as f32`.

`fn arg_of(i int) byte[>..]` returns os argument i as an owned byte array.

`main()`: arguments after the program name are the .glb path, the output .png path, width, height, eye x y z, target x y z, fov_y in radians. With fewer than 11 arguments after the program name it prints a usage line and returns. It sets `gltf::lazy_pixels = true`, loads the model with `gltf::load` and prints `gltf::error()` and returns when that is negative. It opens headless with `gfx::open_headless(w, h)`, adds the asset with `s3d::add` and places it at `gfx::mat4_identity`. `s3d::view` becomes an `s3d::Camera` with those values, up (0,1,0), near = distance(eye, target) * 0.01 and far = distance(eye, target) * 10. `s3d::sun.shadows = false`, `s3d::sun.rgb` is zero, `s3d::sky` is an `s3d::Sky` with zenith, horizon and ground all (0.5,0.5,0.5), ambient 1, draw true. `s3d::look.curve = 1`, `s3d::look.bloom = 0`, `s3d::look.ssao = false`. Argument 12, when present, is a comma-separated feature list, split on commas: a word `env=<path>` calls `envmap::load(<path>)` and prints the path when it fails, and sets `s3d::sky.draw = true`. Add import gfx.envmap. A word `dbg=<n>` sets `s3d::debug_view` to the integer n. It calls `s3d::init(w, h)`, makes `gfx::render_target(w, h, gfx::RGBA8)`, renders 2 frames with `s3d::render`, saves with `gfx::save_png`, prints "vis_frame: " and the saved result and `gfx::driver()`, and calls `gfx::close()`.
